{"type":"message","id":"4f72dfdc","parentId":"ca2b5d5c","timestamp":"2026-06-24T08:28:26.146Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_function_q3cpojnqij3w_1","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[\n  {\n    \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/vhallac/semblr/issues/comments/4785886765\",\n    \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/vhallac/semblr/issues/86#issuecomment-4785886765\",\n    \"issue_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/vhallac/semblr/issues/86\",\n    \"id\": 4785886765,\n    \"node_id\": \"IC_kwDOSoADIs8AAAABHULWLQ\",\n    \"user\": {\n      \"login\": \"vhallac\",\n      \"id\": 93108,\n      \"node_id\": \"MDQ6VXNlcjkzMTA4\",\n      \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/93108?v=4\",\n      \"gravatar_id\": \"\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac\",\n      \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/vhallac\",\n      \"followers_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/followers\",\n      \"following_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/following{/other_user}\",\n      \"gists_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/gists{/gist_id}\",\n      \"starred_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\n      \"subscriptions_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/subscriptions\",\n      \"organizations_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/orgs\",\n      \"repos_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/repos\",\n      \"events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/events{/privacy}\",\n      \"received_events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/received_events\",\n      \"type\": \"User\",\n      \"user_view_type\": \"public\",\n      \"site_admin\": false\n    },\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-06-24T04:32:35Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-06-24T05:23:21Z\",\n    \"body\": \"## Semblr: Multi-Model Routing — MVP Specification\\n\\n### Concept\\n\\nThe LLM self-assesses its generation phase during a response by calling a new `semblr_report_phase` tool. Semblr stores the reported phase and, at agent cycle boundaries (`agent_end`), switches to a different model if the phase warrants it. The idea is simple: let the LLM tell us what kind of work it's doing (\\\"I'm executing a plan\\\" vs \\\"I'm stuck\\\"), and route accordingly. No external classifier, no heuristics — the LLM itself provides the signal.\\n\\n### MVP Scope\\n\\n#### ✅ In the MVP\\n\\n- **One new tool:** `semblr_report_phase` — the LLM calls this to declare its current phase\\n- **Six phases:** `exploring`, `planning`, `executing`, `stuck`, `verifying`, `reporting`\\n- **Two tool parameters:**\\n  - `phase` (required) — one of the six phase literals\\n  - `note` (optional) — a \\\"note to future self\\\" string, marked **EXPERIMENTAL**, passed to the next model if a switch occurs\\n- **Routing at `agent_end` only** — switches happen between agent cycles, not mid-response\\n- **One config toggle:** `semblr.routing.enabled` (default: `false`) — set in settings.json or via `SEMBLR_ROUTING_ENABLED=true` env var\\n- **Switch announcements in output stream** — e.g., `Semblr: switching to anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514 for execution phase`\\n- **Visible errors on switch failure** — if `pi.setModel()` fails, an error is surfaced in the output stream (same style as LLM errors)\\n- **Hardcoded switch limiter:** maximum 3 model switches per agent cycle (not configurable)\\n\\n#### ❌ Explicitly Out of MVP (deferred to enhancement)\\n\\n- No presets (`fast-sonnet`, `dual-model`, `escalation-only`)\\n- No user-facing `phaseModels` config mapping — the phase→model map is hardcoded for MVP\\n- No config knobs: `maxSwitchesPerCycle`, `maxConsecutiveStuck`, `minTurnsPerPhase`, `announceSwitches`, `escalationModel`, `logLevel`\\n- No `/semblr:routing` slash command — routing is enabled/disabled via config file or env var only\\n- No TUI integration beyond output stream messages (no status bar widget, no phase indicators)\\n\\n### Tool Signature\\n\\n```typescript\\n// TypeBox schema for semblr_report_phase\\nType.Object({\\n  phase: Type.Union([\\n    Type.Literal(\\\"exploring\\\"),\\n    Type.Literal(\\\"planning\\\"),\\n    Type.Literal(\\\"executing\\\"),\\n    Type.Literal(\\\"stuck\\\"),\\n    Type.Literal(\\\"verifying\\\"),\\n    Type.Literal(\\\"reporting\\\"),\\n  ], {\\n    description: \\\"Your current generation phase. 'exploring': pulling in external data by reading, searching, etc. 'planning': formulating a plan of response. 'executing': implementing the plan. 'stuck': underspecified, insufficient data — fishing by extensive exploration or guessing. 'verifying': execution done, validating output and created files. 'reporting': done, about to report the final output.\\\"\\n  }),\\n  note: Type.Optional(Type.String({\\n    description: \\\"EXPERIMENTAL: A note to the next model that handles this task. Share context, decisions made, open questions, or suggestions for how to proceed. Keep it concise.\\\"\\n  })),\\n})\\n```\\n\\n**Tool name:** `semblr_report_phase`  \\n**Label (TUI):** `Report Phase`  \\n**promptSnippet:** `Report your current generation phase for multi-model routing`\\n\\n### Why MVP First\\n\\nThere is **no prior art** for LLM self-assessment routing. We don't know:\\n\\n- Whether LLMs will actually call the tool\\n- Whether they'll report accurate phases\\n- Whether phase-based routing produces measurably better outcomes\\n\\nThe MVP's sole purpose is to **gather experimental data**. If LLMs use the tool reliably and routing improves results, the full preset/config system is worth building. If LLMs ignore it or report wrong phases, we haven't wasted time on features nobody will use.\\n\\n### Hardcoded Model Map (MVP)\\n\\n```\\nexploring  → current model (no switch)\\nplanning   → current model (no switch)\\nexecuting  → anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\\nstuck      → anthropic/claude-opus-4-20250514\\nverifying  → current model (no switch)\\nreporting  → current model (no switch)\\n```\\n\\nOnly two phases trigger a switch in MVP. `executing` moves to a fast/cheap worker model. `stuck` escalates to a more capable model. All other phases stay on the current model. This map is hardcoded — no user configuration.\\n\\n### What Happens When\\n\\n1. **User enables routing** via `SEMBLR_ROUTING_ENABLED=true` or `semblr.routing.enabled: true` in settings.json\\n2. **Session starts** — `semblr_report_phase` tool is registered; routing state (switch counter, phase history) is initialized\\n3. **LLM calls `semblr_report_phase`** during its response, e.g., `semblr_report_phase({ phase: \\\"executing\\\", note: \\\"Plan: modify auth.ts, api.ts, models.ts\\\" })`\\n4. **Semblr stores the phase + note** in round state; determines target model from the hardcoded map\\n5. **LLM finishes its response** — the rest of the turn proceeds normally\\n6. **At `agent_end`**, Semblr checks:\\n   - Is routing enabled?\\n   - Was a phase reported this round?\\n   - Is the target model different from the current model?\\n   - Has the switch limit (3 per cycle) been exceeded?\\n7. **If all checks pass** → `pi.setModel(targetModel)` is called, and an announcement is emitted in the output stream: `Semblr: switching to claude-sonnet for execution phase`\\n8. **If `pi.setModel()` fails** → a visible error is surfaced in the output stream (styled like LLM errors), and Semblr stays on the current model\\n9. **Next agent cycle starts** — the new model is in effect; if a note was provided, it is injected into context as a `[PHASE TRANSITION NOTE]` so the new model has continuity\\n10. **If >5 switches in a single agent cycle** → routing is suppressed for the remainder of that cycle and a warning is emitted. We will allow for explore -> plan -> execute -> verify -> report.\\n\",\n    \"author_association\": \"OWNER\",\n    \"pin\": null,\n    \"reactions\": {\n      \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/vhallac/semblr/issues/comments/4785886765/reactions\",\n      \"total_count\": 0,\n      \"+1\": 0,\n      \"-1\": 0,\n      \"laugh\": 0,\n      \"hooray\": 0,\n      \"confused\": 0,\n      \"heart\": 0,\n      \"rocket\": 0,\n      \"eyes\": 0\n    },\n    \"performed_via_github_app\": null\n  },\n  {\n    \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/vhallac/semblr/issues/comments/4786167661\",\n    \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/vhallac/semblr/issues/86#issuecomment-4786167661\",\n    \"issue_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/vhallac/semblr/issues/86\",\n    \"id\": 4786167661,\n    \"node_id\": \"IC_kwDOSoADIs8AAAABHUcfbQ\",\n    \"user\": {\n      \"login\": \"vhallac\",\n      \"id\": 93108,\n      \"node_id\": \"MDQ6VXNlcjkzMTA4\",\n      \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/93108?v=4\",\n      \"gravatar_id\": \"\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac\",\n      \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/vhallac\",\n      \"followers_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/followers\",\n      \"following_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/following{/other_user}\",\n      \"gists_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/gists{/gist_id}\",\n      \"starred_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\n      \"subscriptions_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/subscriptions\",\n      \"organizations_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/orgs\",\n      \"repos_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/repos\",\n      \"events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/events{/privacy}\",\n      \"received_events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/vhallac/received_events\",\n      \"type\": \"User\",\n      \"user_view_type\": \"public\",\n      \"site_admin\": false\n    },\n    \"created_at\": \"2026-06-24T05:26:47Z\",\n    \"updated_at\": \"2026-06-24T05:26:47Z\",\n    \"body\": \"# Ollama-Cloud Model → Phase Mapping for Semblr Multi-Model Routing\\n\\n## Phase Capability Definitions\\n\\nThese definitions capture what each phase demands from a model. They are designed to be\\nreusable — e.g., for prompt engineering the `semblr_report_phase` tool description, for\\nfuture phase tuning, or for evaluating alternative models.\\n\\n### 1. Exploring — *CRUCIAL*\\n**Purpose:** Discover the codebase landscape. Read files, trace dependencies,\\nidentify all relevant code paths, understand project structure and conventions.\\n\\n**Demands on the model:**\\n- **Large context window** (1M ideal) — must hold a broad view of the project as\\n  files are progressively read. A 128K window risks context churn during thorough\\n  exploration.\\n- **Thoroughness over speed** — the model must resist the temptation to stop early.\\n  A file not considered will wreck the entire round. Pattern recognition across\\n  files is essential.\\n- **Low hallucination** — misreading a file, inventing a path, or guessing an API\\n  signature during exploration propagates errors into planning and execution.\\n- **Good tool discipline** — must read files deliberately, follow breadcrumbs\\n  (grep → read → grep), and not skip files out of laziness.\\n- **Speed/cost trade-off:** Exploration is the one phase where you *should* spend\\n  tokens. A missed file costs far more than an extra 50K tokens of reading.\\n\\n**\\\"No switch\\\" note:** Per user directive, exploring uses whichever model the\\nuser started the session with. No automatic switch. This lets the user control\\nthe exploration capability budget.\\n\\n**Recommended starting model:** `deepseek-v4-pro:cloud` (1M context, 1.6T MoE,\\nthree reasoning modes — can go deep when exploration gets tangled).\\nAlternative: `minimax-m3:cloud` (1M context, agentic tuning).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Planning — *Medium*\\n**Purpose:** Compile and distill the exploration findings into a structured,\\nactionable plan. Identify what to change, in what order, with what approach.\\n\\n**Demands on the model:**\\n- **Distillation capability** — must condense pages of exploration notes into a\\n  coherent plan without losing critical details.\\n- **Structured thinking** — produces ordered steps, identifies dependencies, flags\\n  risks. Strong reasoning but not extreme depth needed.\\n- **Moderate context** — 256K+ is sufficient; 1M is a bonus for very large\\n  exploration dumps but not required.\\n- **Speed benefit** — faster planning means faster execution. This is a good\\n  phase to optimize for latency.\\n- **Cost sensitivity:** Medium. Planning is important but typically short (a few\\n  turns). A mid-tier model is appropriate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Executing — *High*\\n**Purpose:** Generate code, apply precise edits, use tools, follow the plan.\\nThis is where the actual work gets done.\\n\\n**Demands on the model:**\\n- **Strong coding capability** — the model must produce correct, idiomatic code\\n  across languages. Benchmarks like SWE-Bench, Terminal-Bench matter.\\n- **Precise tool use** — edit tool, bash, file operations must be used correctly.\\n  Wrong parameters = broken build.\\n- **Instruction following** — the plan exists; the model must execute it without\\n  wandering off into unnecessary refactors or \\\"improvements.\\\"\\n- **Long-horizon reliability** — some execution phases span many tool calls. The\\n  model must maintain quality across the full trajectory.\\n- **Context window:** 1M is a major advantage for large codebases.\\n- **Confirmed model:** `glm-5.2:cloud`\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Stuck — *Critical*\\n**Purpose:** The current approach has failed. The model must diagnose why,\\ngenerate alternative approaches, and unblock progress.\\n\\n**Demands on the model:**\\n- **Creative problem-solving** — must generate *different* approaches, not\\n  variations of the same failed approach.\\n- **Deep reasoning** — must understand complex failure modes, trace through\\n  multi-step causal chains, identify root causes.\\n- **Debugging intuition** — \\\"have you checked X?\\\" instincts. Pattern-matching\\n  against known failure archetypes.\\n- **Proactive initiative** — should not wait for hand-holding. Must propose\\n  concrete next steps, alternative strategies, or suggest gathering more info.\\n- **Autonomous decomposition** — ability to break a stuck problem into\\n  sub-problems and attack them systematically (swarm orchestration is a plus).\\n- **Context window:** 256K is usually sufficient for focused debugging.\\n- **Cost sensitivity:** Low. When you're stuck, you pay whatever it takes to\\n  get unstuck. This is the phase that justifies the most expensive model.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Verifying — *Medium*\\n**Purpose:** Check that the execution produced correct results. Run tests,\\ncompare outputs, validate behavior, identify regressions.\\n\\n**Demands on the model:**\\n- **Methodical thoroughness** — must systematically check each claim, run each\\n  test, compare each output. No shortcuts.\\n- **Strong tool use** — running tests, reading logs, comparing diffs. The model\\n  is primarily orchestrating tools, not generating new code.\\n- **Attention to detail** — must notice small discrepancies (off-by-one, missing\\n  edge case, subtle output difference).\\n- **Moderate context** — 256K is fine for most verification; 1M helps when\\n  verifying changes across many files.\\n- **Speed benefit** — faster verification means faster iteration. A mid-tier\\n  model is appropriate.\\n- **Cost sensitivity:** Medium. Verification is important but mechanical.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. Reporting — *Low*\\n**Purpose:** Summarize what was done, format results for the user, produce\\nclean output.\\n\\n**Demands on the model:**\\n- **Summarization** — condense the round's work into a clear summary.\\n- **Formatting** — produce clean markdown, structured output.\\n- **Minimal reasoning needed** — the heavy thinking is done. This is presentation.\\n- **Context window:** 128K+ is sufficient.\\n- **Cost sensitivity:** High. This is the phase to optimize for cost. A small,\\n  fast 